<p>(Disclaimer: Long time reader but new to the community, so maybe take what I say with a pinch of salt)</p>
<p>I really like the core idea of a mundane software bug causing an anomalous effect, but I found myself not really enjoying this one very much. The execution trivialises the anomaly and there seems to be an undertone of flippancy about the whole thing. Having your computer system, which stores some of the most secret information on the planet, linked with another strikes me as a huge issue. The final addendum really bothered me as well. The problem is getting worse, potentially allowing multiple other universes/timelines access to all stored data, and the response is essentially &quot;meh, it's probably fine&quot;. In the SCPverse, they beat the odds that the first place they were linked to didn't immediately start trying to murder (or worse) everyone. Odds should be that whoever else is getting a look in at their computer systems has some malevolent intent.</p>

